Yes, it is possible to create a library using GraalVM and interact with it from C code.

GraalVM provides a feature called "Native Image" that can compile Java code into a standalone native executable that can be run without a JVM. This feature can be used to create a library that can be linked and used from C code.

To create a native library using GraalVM, you would need to follow these steps:

1. Write the Java code that you want to turn into a library. Make sure that the code does not use any Java-specific libraries or features that are not supported by GraalVM's native image generation.
2. Use the GraalVM native image tool to compile the Java code into a native executable. This will create a shared library that can be used from C code.
3. Write the C code that will interact with the native library. You will need to use the C standard library functions to load and call the functions in the native library.

Here is an example of how to create a native library using GraalVM:

1. Create a Java class with a static method that takes an integer argument and returns a string:
```arduino
public class MyLibrary {
  public static String myMethod(int i) {
    return "Hello from Java: " + i;
  }
}
```
1. Use the GraalVM native image tool to compile the Java code into a native executable:
```arduino
native-image --shared MyLibrary
```
This will create a shared library called "libmylibrary.so" that can be linked from C code.

1. Write the C code that will interact with the native library:
```arduino
#include <stdio.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>

int main() {
  void* handle = dlopen("./libmylibrary.so", RTLD_LAZY);
  if (!handle) {
    printf("Error: %s\n", dlerror());
    return 1;
  }
  typedef const char* (*my_method_t)(int);
  my_method_t my_method = (my_method_t) dlsym(handle, "myMethod");
  if (!my_method) {
    printf("Error: %s\n", dlerror());
    dlclose(handle);
    return 1;
  }
  const char* result = my_method(42);
  printf("%s\n", result);
  dlclose(handle);
  return 0;
}
```
This code uses the `dlopen` and `dlsym` functions from the C standard library to load the shared library and call the `myMethod` function.

Note that you will need to make sure that the GraalVM native image tool is installed on your system and that your C compiler is configured to link against the GraalVM libraries.

