Here is an example of creating a simple website using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

HTML Code:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>My Simple Website</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Welcome to my website!</h1>
    <p>This is a simple website created using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.</p>
    <button id="myButton">Click me!</button>
  </body>
</html>
```
CSS Code:
```css
body {
    background-color: #f2f2f2;
}

h1 {
    text-align: center;
}

#myButton {
    background-color: blue;
    color: white;
    padding: 10px 20px;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
}
```
JavaScript Code:
```javascript
document.getElementById("myButton").addEventListener("click", function(){
    alert("You clicked the button!");
});
```
This website consists of an HTML file, a CSS file, and a JavaScript file. The HTML file provides the structure and content of the website, the CSS file is used to style the website, and the JavaScript file is used to add interactivity to the website.

You need to save the above code in three different files with extension as .html , .css and .js respectively.

Then you need to open the HTML file in a web browser, you will see a simple website with a heading and a button. When you click the button, an alert message will appear.

Yes, option 1 can be made more efficient by only adding the nudge value to the diagonal elements when negative eigenvalues are found. This way, you avoid altering the matrix unnecessarily. Here's an updated version of option 1:
```R
eigen_values <- eigen(K)$values

# Check if there are any negative eigenvalues
if (any(eigen_values < 0)) {
  nudge_value <- 1e-6
  K_nudged <- K + diag(nudge_value, nrow(K))
  result <- rmvnorm(n=1, mean=rep(0, nrow(x)), sigma=K_nudged, method="chol")
} else {
  result <- rmvnorm(n=1, mean=rep(0, nrow(x)), sigma=K, method="chol")
}
```
This way, the nudge value is only added to the diagonal elements when the matrix has negative eigenvalues. If the matrix is already positive semi-definite, the original matrix K will be used directly.

