Sure, here is an example of how to use OpenCV.js to detect edges on an uploaded image using the HTML5 Canvas and File Reader APIs:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>OpenCV.js Edge Detection Example</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>OpenCV.js Edge Detection Example</h1>
    <input type="file" id="imageInput" accept="image/*">
    <canvas id="imageCanvas"></canvas>
    <canvas id="edgeCanvas"></canvas>
    <script async src="https://docs.opencv.org/master/opencv.js" onload="onOpenCvReady();" type="text/javascript"></script>
    <script type="text/javascript">
      // load OpenCV
      function onOpenCvReady() {
        // set up the input and output canvases
        var imageCanvas = document.getElementById('imageCanvas');
        var edgeCanvas = document.getElementById('edgeCanvas');
        var imageContext = imageCanvas.getContext('2d');
        var edgeContext = edgeCanvas.getContext('2d');

        // handle file input change
        var imageInput = document.getElementById('imageInput');
        imageInput.addEventListener('change', function() {
          // load the image from the input file
          var file = imageInput.files[0];
          var reader = new FileReader();
          reader.onload = function() {
            // create an image object and draw it on the canvas
            var image = new Image();
            image.onload = function() {
              imageCanvas.width = image.width;
              imageCanvas.height = image.height;
              imageContext.drawImage(image, 0, 0);

              // convert the canvas to a Mat object for edge detection
              var src = cv.imread(imageCanvas);
              var dst = new cv.Mat();
              cv.Canny(src, dst, 50, 100);

              // draw the edge image on the output canvas
              edgeCanvas.width = dst.cols;
              edgeCanvas.height = dst.rows;
              cv.imshow(edgeCanvas, dst);

              // free memory
              src.delete();
              dst.delete();
            }
            image.src = reader.result;
          }
          reader.readAsDataURL(file);
        });
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```
This example uses the File Reader API to load an image file from the user's computer, then converts the canvas to a Mat object and uses the Canny edge detection algorithm to generate an edge image. Finally, it displays the edge image on a separate canvas element.

Note that this example assumes that OpenCV.js is hosted on a remote server, but you can also download and host it locally if you prefer.

