```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>OpenCV.js Object Detection Example</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>OpenCV.js Object Detection Example</h1>
    <input type="file" id="imageInput" accept="image/*">
    <canvas id="imageCanvas"></canvas>
    <canvas id="resultCanvas"></canvas>
    <script async src="https://docs.opencv.org/master/opencv.js" onload="onOpenCvReady();" type="text/javascript"></script>
    <script type="text/javascript">
      // load OpenCV
      function onOpenCvReady() {
        // set up the input and output canvases
        var imageCanvas = document.getElementById('imageCanvas');
        var resultCanvas = document.getElementById('resultCanvas');
        var imageContext = imageCanvas.getContext('2d');
        var resultContext = resultCanvas.getContext('2d');

        // handle file input change
        var imageInput = document.getElementById('imageInput');
        imageInput.addEventListener('change', function() {
          // load the image from the input file
          var file = imageInput.files[0];
          var reader = new FileReader();
          reader.onload = function() {
            // create an image object and draw it on the canvas
            var image = new Image();
            image.onload = function() {
              imageCanvas.width = image.width;
              imageCanvas.height = image.height;
              imageContext.drawImage(image, 0, 0);

              // convert the canvas to a Mat object for object detection
              var src = cv.imread(imageCanvas);
              var dst = new cv.Mat();

              // convert the image to grayscale
              cv.cvtColor(src, dst, cv.COLOR_RGBA2GRAY);

              // detect objects using Haar cascades
              var classifier = new cv.CascadeClassifier();
              classifier.load('haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml');
              var faces = new cv.RectVector();
              classifier.detectMultiScale(dst, faces, 1.1, 3, 0);

              // draw the results on the output canvas
              resultCanvas.width = imageCanvas.width;
              resultCanvas.height = imageCanvas.height;
              resultContext.drawImage(imageCanvas, 0, 0);
              resultContext.lineWidth = 3;
              resultContext.strokeStyle = '#FF0000';
              for (var i = 0; i < faces.size(); i++) {
                var face = faces.get(i);
                resultContext.strokeRect(face.x, face.y, face.width, face.height);
              }

              // free memory
              src.delete();
              dst.delete();
              classifier.delete();
              faces.delete();
            }
            image.src = reader.result;
          }
          reader.readAsDataURL(file);
        });
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```
This example uses the File Reader API to load an image file from the user's computer, then converts the canvas to a Mat object and applies a grayscale conversion. It then uses a pre-trained Haar Cascade classifier to detect objects (in this case, faces) in the image, and draws bounding boxes around them on a separate canvas element.

Note that this example assumes that the Haar Cascade XML file is hosted in the same directory as the HTML file, but you can also download it and host it elsewhere if you prefer.

