PHP provides a variety of built-in functions for manipulating strings. Here are six common string manipulation functions in PHP:

1. `strlen()`: Returns the length of a string.
```
$string = "Hello, World!";
$length = strlen($string);
echo $length;
```
2. `strtoupper()`: Converts all characters in a string to uppercase.
```
$string = "Hello, World!";
$uppercase = strtoupper($string);
echo $uppercase;
```
3. `strtolower()`: Converts all characters in a string to lowercase.
```
$string = "Hello, World!";
$lowercase = strtolower($string);
echo $lowercase;
```
4. `substr()`: Returns a portion of a string.
```
$string = "Hello, World!";
$substring = substr($string, 7, 5);
echo $substring;
```
5. `str_replace()`: Replaces all occurrences of a search string with a replacement string.
```
$string = "Hello, World!";
$newString = str_replace("World", "PHP", $string);
echo $newString;
```
6. `strpos()`: Finds the position of the first occurrence of a substring in a string.
```
$string = "Hello, World!";
$position = strpos($string, "World");
echo $position;
```
These are just a few of the many string manipulation functions available in PHP. Other useful functions include `trim()`, `str_split()`, `strrev()`, `str_pad()`, `str_repeat()` and many more.

